  "text_sanskrit": "अथ चैनं नित्यजातं नित्यं वा मन्यसे मृतम् । तथापि त्वं महाबाहो नैवं शोचितुमर्हसि ॥ २-२६ ॥",
  "itrans": "atha chaina.n nityajAta.n nitya.n vA manyase mR^itam . tathApi tvaM mahAbAho naiva.n shochitumarhasi .. 2-26 ..",
  "translation": "Or if thou thinkest of him as ever born or as ever dead, even then, O mighty armed, it is not fit that thou grievest. (26) \nV. Ever (Nitya) here adds emphasis. The first clause means ‘If thou shouldst think of him as having birth alone’. The second means ‘Or if thou shouldst think of him as having death alone, i.e., if once dead he is dead for ever). In either case no reason for grief is seen, for each is inevitable according to his own hypothesis. (26.) \nA. In the preceding verse the first half states the doubt of Arjuna, and the second half his grief is groundless. But how groundless, it has not been stated. The next verse gives the reason with the conclusion that birth and death inevitably succeed each other till Moksha is obtained.",
